    explanation: "The cigar is a pleasure with no connection whatever to gender. There is no essential difference between the sexes in how taste and aroma are perceived or enjoyed; the notion that cigars are 'a man's thing' is merely an assumption shaped by history and culture. Trace the history and women smokers existed everywhere as bearers of tobacco culture, and records of women enjoying cigars in society and in artistic circles are far from scarce. Today, alongside the many women enthusiasts, women increasingly lead the industry worldwide — as tasters and blenders, as heads of tobacco farms and manufacturers, and as owners and sommeliers of cigar shops and lounges. Celebrated women roller-makers and brand founders who craft their own blends have emerged, earning high marks at international competitions. Recent years have also seen cigar events and communities for women, and suggestions of smaller, lighter vitolas aimed at women, spreading in many places and broadening the base of cigar culture. The essence lies not in who smokes, but in how one savours and enjoys; there is no reason anywhere to draw a line by gender."
